  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 546 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nBy Saturday, the next frontal boundary moves into western GA just \nbefore daybreak. Models still indicate the system to be pretty well \norganized by the time it reaches the GA/AL border so it's something \nwe'll need to keep an eye on the threat it poses over the next few \ndays. The front stalls out and sets us up to return to a pattern of \nafternoon thunderstorms. With it being a source of lift through the \nweekend and into the start of next week, a few storms in the \nafternoon could become strong to severe.\n\nTemperatures through the rest of the long term should remain below \nheat advisory criteria, though the trend is for them to gradually \nincrease through next week. Expect highs in the upper 80s and low \n90s; regulated in part by at least some cooling during the \nafternoon.",
